Chapter: Arm64 LIR Register File

Section: The Register File

 An abstract allocator register `k` (a `LocReg k`) is the physical register
 `a64-lir-phys[k]`. A spill slot `k` (a `LocSlot k`) is the stack cell
 `[SP + k*8]`.

 **`[0, ncallee)` is `x19` through `x27`, nine callee-saved registers.** A
 value live across a call is confined here by the allocator's crossing
 analysis, and the callee preserves them. `x28` is callee-saved on AAPCS and
 is NOT in the file: it is the bump allocator (`a64-heap-reg`), the ARM64
 counterpart of x86-64's `R10`, and handing it to the allocator would hand
 out the heap.

 **Index `ncallee` alone is `x9`.** This is the slot x86-64 gives `RAX`: the
 register a value that does not cross a call prefers, and the one a leaf
 skips. It is deliberately NOT `x0`. On x86-64, `RAX` is the return register
 and is not an argument register, so one index can carry both roles. On ARM64
 `x0` is the return register AND argument zero, and giving one physical
 register two abstract indices would let the allocator believe it holds two
 independent values. `x9` is caller-saved, is not an argument register, and
 has no other role in this backend, so it takes the non-crosser role cleanly
 and `x0` keeps its single meaning as argument zero.

 **`[nargregs, nregs)` is `x0` through `x7`, the argument registers in
 argument order.** This is what makes a leaf's entry moves disappear: the
 parameters are scanned first against a free file, so parameter `i` is handed
 `a64-lir-phys[10 + i]`, which is the register it already arrived in, and the
 move is `mov x0, x0` and is elided.

  a64-lir-phys : List Integer = [19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 9, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]

 `diva` and `divb` are both -1. ARM64 divides with `sdiv` and takes the
 remainder with `msub`, both three-operand, so division fixes no register.
 `LirTargets.codex` records that this axis had a known trap behind it -- a
 consumer that wrote `list-set-at occ (0 - 1)` would corrupt the verifier's
 occupant map rather than exclude nothing -- and that the probe descriptors
 asked the question and found every site guarded. This is the first shipping
 target to answer -1, so that guarding is now load-bearing rather than
 hypothetical.

 `budget` is 24, above the 18 allocatable registers, because it sizes the
 verifier's occupant array and slack there is cheaper than an overflow.

  lir-target-a64 : LirTarget = LirTarget {
   ncallee = 9,
   nargregs = 10,
   nregs = 18,
   diva = 0 - 1,
   divb = 0 - 1,
   budget = 24
  }

  a64-lir-slot-off : Integer -> Integer
  a64-lir-slot-off (k) = k * 8

 The scratch pair, never handed out by the allocator. `x10` holds a spilled
 destination or an oversized immediate; `x11` holds a spilled or oversized
 second operand. Both are caller-saved, both are consumed inside a single
 instruction's selection, and neither is ever live across a call.

  a64-lir-scratch-reg : Integer = 10

  a64-lir-scratch2 : Integer = 11

 The number of callee-saved registers a function must save and restore: one
 past the highest `LocReg` index that is callee-saved (an abstract index below
 `ncallee`). Index 9 (`x9`) and the argument window above it are caller-saved
 and are never saved. Because the allocator hands out the callee pool
 lowest-free, the used callee indices are a prefix, so saving `[0, this)`
 saves exactly the ones in use.

  a64-lir-nused-callee : List Location, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  a64-lir-nused-callee (assign) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else when (list-at assign i)
    is LocReg (r) -> a64-lir-nused-callee assign (i + 1) n (if (r < (lir-target-a64.ncallee)) & ((r + 1) > acc) then r + 1 else acc)
    is LocSlot (s) -> a64-lir-nused-callee assign (i + 1) n acc

  a64-lir-nslots : List Location, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  a64-lir-nslots (assign) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else when (list-at assign i)
    is LocSlot (s) -> a64-lir-nslots assign (i + 1) n (if (s + 1) > acc then s + 1 else acc)
    is LocReg (r) -> a64-lir-nslots assign (i + 1) n acc

Section: The Frame

 The frame is one stack adjustment holding two regions. The spill slots sit at
 the bottom, so slot `k` stays at `[SP + k*8]` and `a64-lir-slot-off` needs no
 base added to it; the saved callee-saved registers sit above them. Nothing in
 a whitelisted body moves SP, so both addresses are stable from the prologue to
 the epilogue.

 The size is rounded up to sixteen bytes because AArch64 faults on an
 SP-relative access when SP is not sixteen-byte aligned, which is a rule about
 the stack pointer rather than about the access, and so applies to a frame this
 small exactly as it does to a large one.

 **The link register is saved exactly when the body contains a call**, because
 that is exactly when something writes it: `bl` puts the return address in
 `x30`, and a function that returns after making a call and did not save it
 returns to its own callee's return site. A leaf saves nothing and its frame is
 the byte-identical one step 4d emitted.

  A64LirFrame = record {
   nslots : Integer,
   nused : Integer,
   haslr : Boolean
  }

  a64-lir-frame-cells : A64LirFrame -> Integer
  a64-lir-frame-cells (fr) = (fr.nslots) + (fr.nused) + (if fr.haslr then 1 else 0)

  a64-lir-frame-bytes : A64LirFrame -> Integer
  a64-lir-frame-bytes (fr) = (((a64-lir-frame-cells fr * 8) + 15) / 16) * 16

  a64-lir-save-off : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  a64-lir-save-off (nslots) (i) = (nslots * 8) + (i * 8)

  a64-lir-lr-off : A64LirFrame -> Integer
  a64-lir-lr-off (fr) = ((fr.nslots) + (fr.nused)) * 8

 Callee-saved index `i` is `a64-lir-phys[i]`, which the register file puts at
 `x19 + i` for the nine indices below `ncallee`. The allocator hands that pool
 out lowest-free, so the used indices are a prefix and saving `[0, nused)`
 saves exactly the registers the body will write.

  a64-lir-save-regs : A64State, Integer, Integer, Integer -> A64State
  a64-lir-save-regs (st) (i) (nused) (nslots) =
   if i >= nused then st
   else a64-lir-save-regs (a64-emit st (arm64-str (list-at a64-lir-phys i) a64-sp (a64-lir-save-off nslots i))) (i + 1) nused nslots

  a64-lir-restore-regs : A64State, Integer, Integer, Integer -> A64State
  a64-lir-restore-regs (st) (i) (nused) (nslots) =
   if i >= nused then st
   else a64-lir-restore-regs (a64-emit st (arm64-ldr (list-at a64-lir-phys i) a64-sp (a64-lir-save-off nslots i))) (i + 1) nused nslots

  a64-lir-emit-prologue : A64State, A64LirFrame -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-prologue (st) (fr) =
   let frame = a64-lir-frame-bytes fr
   in if frame == 0 then st
      else let st1 = a64-lir-save-regs (a64-emit st (arm64-sub-imm a64-sp a64-sp frame)) 0 (fr.nused) (fr.nslots)
      in if fr.haslr then a64-emit st1 (arm64-str a64-lr a64-sp (a64-lir-lr-off fr)) else st1

 The result is already in `x0` when the epilogue runs, and neither the saved
 pool nor the link register is `x0`, so restoring cannot disturb it.

  a64-lir-emit-epilogue : A64State, A64LirFrame -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-epilogue (st) (fr) =
   let frame = a64-lir-frame-bytes fr
   in if frame == 0 then st
      else let st1 = if fr.haslr then a64-emit st (arm64-ldr a64-lr a64-sp (a64-lir-lr-off fr)) else st
      in a64-emit (a64-lir-restore-regs st1 0 (fr.nused) (fr.nslots)) (arm64-add-imm a64-sp a64-sp frame)

Section: Entry Moves

 These run after the prologue, which is required in both directions: a store
 into a spill slot needs the frame to exist, and a move into a callee-saved
 register must not happen before that register has been saved.

  a64-lir-emv-phys : Integer -> Integer
  a64-lir-emv-phys (i) =
   if i >= (lir-target-a64.nregs) then a64-lir-scratch-reg else list-at a64-lir-phys i

  a64-lir-emit-emv : A64State, LirEmv -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-emv (st) (step) =
   when step
    is EmvMove (d) (s) ->
     let pd = a64-lir-emv-phys d
     in let ps = a64-lir-emv-phys s
     in if pd == ps then st else a64-emit st (arm64-mov pd ps)
    is EmvStore (k) (s) -> a64-emit st (arm64-str (a64-lir-emv-phys s) a64-sp (a64-lir-slot-off k))

  a64-lir-emit-emvs : A64State, List LirEmv, Integer, Integer -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-emvs (st) (plan) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then st
   else a64-lir-emit-emvs (a64-lir-emit-emv st (list-at plan i)) plan (i + 1) n

  a64-lir-emit-entry : A64State, LirFunc, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-entry (st) (f) (assign) =
   let plan = lir-emv-for lir-target-a64 f assign
   in a64-lir-emit-emvs st plan 0 (list-length plan)

Section: The Construct Whitelist

 **This is the first construct that consumes the register assignment**, and
 therefore the first place a descriptor error can reach an emitted byte. It is
 also where the design doc's first open prediction is answered: x86-64's
 selector carries aliasing machinery (`lir-alias-r`, `lir-sel-bin-aliased`)
 because its ALU is two-operand and `op dst, src` destroys `dst`. **ARM64 is
 three-operand and needs none of it.** `add xd, xn, xm` reads both sources
 before it writes, so the case x86 has to detect -- the destination register
 already holding the right operand -- is simply correct here with no special
 case at all. That is not a shortcut taken; it is a machine difference, and it
 is why this arm is shorter than its x86 counterpart rather than longer.

  a64-lir-op-ok : LirOp -> Boolean
  a64-lir-op-ok (op) =
   when op
    is LoAdd -> True
    is LoSub -> True
    is LoMul -> True
    is LoRem -> False
    is LoDiv -> False

 **Only an eight-byte load is admitted, and the reason is the encoder rather
 than the selector.** A field's width comes from the shared width table, so a
 pointer, an unbounded integer and a sum payload are all eight bytes and a
 bounded integer like `Integer between 0 and 255` is narrower. `arm64-ldr`
 covers the eight-byte case exactly; the narrow cases need `ldrsw`, `ldrh`,
 `ldrsh` and `ldrsb`, none of which this encoder has, and adding four
 encodings is a separate change with its own sensitivity runs. A narrow load
 declines to the tree emitter, which reads it correctly today.

 **The offset gate cannot fire, and it is here because the encoder masks.**
 Fields pack widest-first from offset 8, so every eight-byte field lands at
 `8 + k*8` and the tag lands at 0 -- each of them a non-negative multiple of
 eight. But `arm64-ldr`'s unsigned form scales its immediate by eight and
 masks it to twelve bits, so an offset that was not a multiple of eight would
 be silently rounded down and one past 32760 silently truncated, and both are
 miscompiles rather than diagnostics. This is the same reasoning that bounds
 the frame at 4095 bytes in `a64-lir-alloc-ok`, and the same choice: refuse
 the shape rather than trust an encoder that cannot refuse it.

 `codex/test/lir-load-cross` pins the eight-byte shapes by answers and pins the
 decline as well: `Bytes` carries two one-byte fields, so they are ADJACENT at
 offsets 8 and 9, and an eight-byte load at 8 would answer `a + b * 256` while
 one at 9 would answer the same again (9/8 is 1). A gate that let them through
 changes both answers rather than merely the instructions.

  a64-lir-load-ok : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-load-ok (off) (w) =
   if w /= 8 then False
   else if off < 0 then False
   else if ((off / 8) * 8) /= off then False
   else off <= 32760

  a64-lir-whitelisted : LirInsn -> Boolean
  a64-lir-whitelisted (insn) =
   when insn
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> a64-lir-op-ok op
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> True
    is LiCmp (l) (r) -> True
    is LiTest (l) (r) -> True
    is LiBranch (c) (t) (fl) -> True
    is LiJump (b) -> True
    is LiRet (v) -> True
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> True
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> a64-lir-load-ok off w
    is otherwise -> False

  a64-lir-known-arity : List Text, List Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  a64-lir-known-arity (names) (arities) (target) (i) =
   if i >= list-length names then 0 - 1
   else if list-at names i == target then list-at arities i
   else a64-lir-known-arity names arities target (i + 1)

  a64-lir-call-ok : A64State, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-call-ok (st) (name) (nargs) =
   if nargs > 8 then False
   else a64-lir-known-arity (st.func-names) (st.func-arities) name 0 == nargs

  a64-lir-calls-ok-insns : A64State, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-calls-ok-insns (st) (insns) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else when (list-at insns i)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> if a64-lir-call-ok st name (list-length args) then a64-lir-calls-ok-insns st insns (i + 1) n else False
    is otherwise -> a64-lir-calls-ok-insns st insns (i + 1) n

  a64-lir-calls-ok : A64State, List LirBlock, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-calls-ok (st) (blocks) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else if a64-lir-calls-ok-insns st ((list-at blocks i).insns) 0 (list-length ((list-at blocks i).insns))
        then a64-lir-calls-ok st blocks (i + 1) n
        else False

  a64-lir-has-call-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-has-call-insns (insns) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else when (list-at insns i)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> True
    is otherwise -> a64-lir-has-call-insns insns (i + 1) n

  a64-lir-has-call : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-has-call (blocks) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else if a64-lir-has-call-insns ((list-at blocks i).insns) 0 (list-length ((list-at blocks i).insns)) then True
   else a64-lir-has-call blocks (i + 1) n

  a64-lir-insns-ok : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-insns-ok (insns) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else if a64-lir-whitelisted (list-at insns i) then a64-lir-insns-ok insns (i + 1) n
   else False

  a64-lir-blocks-ok : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-blocks-ok (blocks) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else if a64-lir-insns-ok ((list-at blocks i).insns) 0 (list-length ((list-at blocks i).insns))
        then a64-lir-blocks-ok blocks (i + 1) n
        else False

 The result of a zero-instruction function is either an immediate or a vreg,
 and with no instructions **every vreg is a parameter** -- vregs `0 ..
 nparams-1` are the parameters by construction, and nothing else ever defined
 one. So the result operand is a parameter index, and a parameter index is an
 argument register: parameter `i` arrives in `x_i` under the ABI, whatever the
 allocator later assigns it.

 The eight-argument bound is the ABI's: a ninth parameter arrives on the
 caller's stack, not in a register.

  a64-lir-result-ok : LirValue, Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-result-ok (v) (nparams) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> True
    is LvReg (r) -> if r < nparams then r < 8 else False

  a64-lir-selects : LirFunc -> Boolean
  a64-lir-selects (f) =
   if (f.nparams) > 8 then False
   else if a64-lir-zero-insn f
        then a64-lir-result-ok (f.result) (f.nparams)
        else a64-lir-blocks-ok (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks))

Section: Dispatch

  - a function with more than eight parameters is declined, because a ninth
    arrives on the caller's stack rather than in a register (x86-64 says six
    for the same reason at four argument registers);
  - a body the compiler's lowering declines is declined here too;
  - a body holding any construct outside the whitelist is declined, which today
    is anything but a zero-instruction function, straight-line
    add/subtract/multiply, a comparison, a branch, a return, or a call;
  - and a body holding a call whose target this module has not already emitted
    with the same arity is declined, because an ARM64 call that does not
    resolve is patched into nothing rather than reported.

  A64LirTry = record {
   handled : Boolean,
   state : A64State
  }

  a64-lir-not-handled : A64State -> A64LirTry
  a64-lir-not-handled (st) = A64LirTry { handled = False, state = st }

  a64-lir-emit-result-param : A64State, LirValue -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-result-param (st) (v) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> a64-emit-li st a64-x0 k
    is LvReg (r) -> if r == 0 then st else a64-emit st (arm64-mov a64-x0 (a64-x0 + r))

 The allocator never hands out `x10` or `x11`, so a loaded or materialised
 operand cannot land on top of a live value.

  A64LirSrc = record {
   state : A64State,
   reg : Integer
  }

  a64-lir-src-loc : A64State, Location, Integer -> A64LirSrc
  a64-lir-src-loc (st) (loc) (scratch) =
   when loc
    is LocReg (pr) -> A64LirSrc { state = st, reg = list-at a64-lir-phys pr }
    is LocSlot (k) -> A64LirSrc { state = a64-emit st (arm64-ldr scratch a64-sp (a64-lir-slot-off k)), reg = scratch }

  a64-lir-src : A64State, LirValue, List Location, Integer -> A64LirSrc
  a64-lir-src (st) (v) (assign) (scratch) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> A64LirSrc { state = a64-emit-li st scratch k, reg = scratch }
    is LvReg (r) -> a64-lir-src-loc st (list-at assign r) scratch

 The ALU itself. Three operands, so the destination is written and never read,
 and `LoAdd`/`LoSub`/`LoMul` map one to one onto `add`/`sub`/`mul`.

  a64-lir-emit-alu : A64State, LirOp, Integer, Integer, Integer -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-alu (st) (op) (rd) (rn) (rm) =
   when op
    is LoAdd -> a64-emit st (arm64-add rd rn rm)
    is LoSub -> a64-emit st (arm64-sub rd rn rm)
    is LoMul -> a64-emit st (arm64-mul rd rn rm)
    is LoRem -> st
    is LoDiv -> st

  a64-lir-dst-reg : Location -> Integer
  a64-lir-dst-reg (loc) =
   when loc
    is LocReg (pr) -> list-at a64-lir-phys pr
    is LocSlot (k) -> a64-lir-scratch-reg

  a64-lir-store-dst : A64State, Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-store-dst (st) (loc) =
   when loc
    is LocReg (pr) -> st
    is LocSlot (k) -> a64-emit st (arm64-str a64-lir-scratch-reg a64-sp (a64-lir-slot-off k))

  a64-lir-emit-bin : A64State, LirOp, Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-bin (st) (op) (dst) (l) (r) (assign) =
   let sl = a64-lir-src st l assign a64-lir-scratch-reg
   in let sr = a64-lir-src (sl.state) r assign a64-lir-scratch2
   in let d = list-at assign dst
   in a64-lir-store-dst (a64-lir-emit-alu (sr.state) op (a64-lir-dst-reg d) (sl.reg) (sr.reg)) d

  a64-lir-load-into : A64State, Integer, LirValue, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-load-into (st) (dr) (src) (assign) =
   when src
    is LvImm (k) -> a64-emit-li st dr k
    is LvReg (r) ->
     when (list-at assign r)
      is LocReg (pr) -> let p = list-at a64-lir-phys pr in if p == dr then st else a64-emit st (arm64-mov dr p)
      is LocSlot (k) -> a64-emit st (arm64-ldr dr a64-sp (a64-lir-slot-off k))

  a64-lir-emit-mov : A64State, Integer, LirValue, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-mov (st) (dst) (src) (assign) =
   when (list-at assign dst)
    is LocReg (pr) -> a64-lir-load-into st (list-at a64-lir-phys pr) src assign
    is LocSlot (k) -> a64-emit (a64-lir-load-into st a64-lir-scratch-reg src assign) (arm64-str a64-lir-scratch-reg a64-sp (a64-lir-slot-off k))

 The base is a pointer the allocator placed, so it resolves through
 `a64-lir-src` into the SECOND scratch, leaving the first free for a spilled
 destination -- the same division of the pair that the arithmetic arm makes and
 that x86-64 makes at this instruction. A destination that IS the base register
 is safe without a third: `ldr xd, [xn, #off]` computes its address before it
 writes, so the write cannot disturb the address it was reading from.

  a64-lir-emit-load : A64State, Integer, LirValue, Integer, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-load (st) (dst) (base) (off) (assign) =
   let bb = a64-lir-src st base assign a64-lir-scratch2
   in let d = list-at assign dst
   in a64-lir-store-dst (a64-emit (bb.state) (arm64-ldr (a64-lir-dst-reg d) (bb.reg) off)) d

 A comparison sets the flags and defines nothing. `arm64-cmp` is `subs xzr`, so
 the operands are read and the result discarded exactly as x86's `cmp` does.
 An immediate right operand takes the immediate form when it fits ARM64's
 twelve-bit unsigned field, and is materialised into the second scratch when it
 does not, which is the same fallback the arithmetic arm uses for the same
 reason.

  a64-lir-cmp-imm-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  a64-lir-cmp-imm-ok (k) = if k < 0 then False else k <= 4095

  a64-lir-emit-cmp : A64State, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-cmp (st) (l) (r) (assign) =
   let sl = a64-lir-src st l assign a64-lir-scratch-reg
   in when r
    is LvImm (k) ->
     if a64-lir-cmp-imm-ok k then a64-emit (sl.state) (arm64-cmp-imm (sl.reg) k)
     else let sr = a64-lir-src (sl.state) r assign a64-lir-scratch2
     in a64-emit (sr.state) (arm64-cmp (sl.reg) (sr.reg))
    is LvReg (vr) ->
     let sr = a64-lir-src (sl.state) r assign a64-lir-scratch2
     in a64-emit (sr.state) (arm64-cmp (sl.reg) (sr.reg))

 A test is `LiCmp`'s bitwise-AND sibling: it ANDs its two operands, sets the
 flags from the result, and defines nothing. `arm64-tst` is `ands xzr`, which
 sets Z exactly as x86's `test` does, so a following `br eq` tests
 `(l & r) == 0`. It appears where the compiler fuses a power-of-two remainder
 against zero -- `math-mod n 2 == 0` becomes `test v0 #1`, `math-mod n 4 == 0`
 becomes `test v0 #3` -- and it only reaches this selector at all because the
 IR wire is now inlined; before that the remainder stayed a call.

 Unlike the compare, the immediate is ALWAYS materialised into the second
 scratch rather than taking an immediate form. `tst Xn, #imm` on AArch64 is the
 logical-bitmask encoding (N:immr:imms), and while the fused masks 1, 3, 7 are
 valid bitmask immediates, encoding them is fiddly and the register form is
 always correct at the cost of one `li`. That the operands are read before the
 flags are set means the scratch load cannot disturb the comparison.

  a64-lir-emit-test : A64State, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-test (st) (l) (r) (assign) =
   let sl = a64-lir-src st l assign a64-lir-scratch-reg
   in let sr = a64-lir-src (sl.state) r assign a64-lir-scratch2
   in a64-emit (sr.state) (arm64-tst (sl.reg) (sr.reg))

 The six conditions, and their inverses. AArch64 numbers them EQ 0, NE 1,
 GE 10, LT 11, GT 12, LE 13, and the inverse of each is the one a fall-through
 branch takes when the layout puts the true successor next.

  a64-lir-cond-code : LirCond -> Integer
  a64-lir-cond-code (c) =
   when c
    is LcEq -> 0
    is LcNe -> 1
    is LcLt -> 11
    is LcGt -> 12
    is LcLe -> 13
    is LcGe -> 10

  a64-lir-cond-inv : LirCond -> Integer
  a64-lir-cond-inv (c) =
   when c
    is LcEq -> 1
    is LcNe -> 0
    is LcLt -> 10
    is LcGt -> 13
    is LcLe -> 12
    is LcGe -> 11

  A64LirFix = record {
   at : Integer,
   target : Integer,
   cond : Integer
  }

  A64LirCtx = record {
   state : A64State,
   boffs : List Integer,
   fixups : List A64LirFix
  }

  a64-lir-ctx-st : A64LirCtx, A64State -> A64LirCtx
  a64-lir-ctx-st (cx) (st) = A64LirCtx { state = st, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = cx.fixups }

  a64-lir-fix : A64LirCtx, A64State, Integer, Integer, Integer -> A64LirCtx
  a64-lir-fix (cx) (st) (at) (target) (cond) =
   A64LirCtx { state = st, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = list-push (cx.fixups) (A64LirFix { at = at, target = target, cond = cond }) }

  a64-lir-emit-branch : A64LirCtx, LirCond, Integer, Integer, Integer -> A64LirCtx
  a64-lir-emit-branch (cx) (c) (tblk) (fblk) (nextid) =
   if fblk == nextid
   then let at = ((cx.state).insn-count)
        in a64-lir-fix cx (a64-emit (cx.state) (arm64-b-cond (a64-lir-cond-code c) 0)) at tblk (a64-lir-cond-code c)
   else if tblk == nextid
   then let at = ((cx.state).insn-count)
        in a64-lir-fix cx (a64-emit (cx.state) (arm64-b-cond (a64-lir-cond-inv c) 0)) at fblk (a64-lir-cond-inv c)
   else let at1 = ((cx.state).insn-count)
        in let cx1 = a64-lir-fix cx (a64-emit (cx.state) (arm64-b-cond (a64-lir-cond-code c) 0)) at1 tblk (a64-lir-cond-code c)
        in let at2 = ((cx1.state).insn-count)
        in a64-lir-fix cx1 (a64-emit (cx1.state) (arm64-b 0)) at2 fblk (0 - 1)

  a64-lir-emit-jump : A64LirCtx, Integer, Integer -> A64LirCtx
  a64-lir-emit-jump (cx) (b) (nextid) =
   if b == nextid then cx
   else let at = ((cx.state).insn-count)
        in a64-lir-fix cx (a64-emit (cx.state) (arm64-b 0)) at b (0 - 1)

  a64-lir-emit-ret : A64State, LirValue, List Location, A64LirFrame -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-ret (st) (v) (assign) (fr) =
   a64-emit (a64-lir-emit-epilogue (a64-lir-emit-result-alloc st v assign) fr) arm64-ret

  a64-lir-emit-amv : A64State, LirAmv, List LirValue -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-amv (st) (step) (argvals) =
   when step
    is AmvMove (d) (s) ->
     let pd = a64-lir-emv-phys d
     in let ps = a64-lir-emv-phys s
     in if pd == ps then st else a64-emit st (arm64-mov pd ps)
    is AmvLoad (d) (k) -> a64-emit st (arm64-ldr (a64-lir-emv-phys d) a64-sp (a64-lir-slot-off k))
    is AmvImm (d) (a) ->
     when (list-at argvals a)
      is LvImm (k) -> a64-emit-li st (a64-lir-emv-phys d) k
      is LvReg (r) -> st

  a64-lir-emit-amvs : A64State, List LirAmv, List LirValue, Integer, Integer -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-amvs (st) (plan) (argvals) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then st
   else a64-lir-emit-amvs (a64-lir-emit-amv st (list-at plan i) argvals) plan argvals (i + 1) n

 The result move has to happen before anything else writes `x0`, and nothing
 does: the next instruction is whatever the block holds, and a second call in
 the same block places its own arguments only after this move has run. A
 destination that IS `x0` -- abstract index `nargregs`, which the allocator
 hands to a value that does not cross a call -- emits nothing.

 Values live across this call are in the callee-saved pool or in spill slots,
 which is the crossing analysis in the descriptor doing its job: `ncallee` is 9
 and `x0` through `x7` sit above it, so the allocator will not leave a
 crossing value in an argument register for the callee to destroy.

  a64-lir-emit-call : A64State, Integer, Text, List LirValue, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-call (st) (dst) (name) (args) (assign) =
   let plan = lir-amv-for lir-target-a64 args assign
   in let st1 = a64-lir-emit-amvs st plan args 0 (list-length plan)
   in let st2 = a64-emit-call-to st1 name
   in when (list-at assign dst)
    is LocReg (pr) ->
     let p = list-at a64-lir-phys pr
     in if p == a64-x0 then st2 else a64-emit st2 (arm64-mov p a64-x0)
    is LocSlot (k) -> a64-emit st2 (arm64-str a64-x0 a64-sp (a64-lir-slot-off k))

  a64-lir-emit-insn : A64LirCtx, LirInsn, List Location, A64LirFrame, Integer -> A64LirCtx
  a64-lir-emit-insn (cx) (ins) (assign) (fr) (nextid) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> a64-lir-ctx-st cx (a64-lir-emit-bin (cx.state) op dst l r assign)
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> a64-lir-ctx-st cx (a64-lir-emit-mov (cx.state) dst src assign)
    is LiCmp (l) (r) -> a64-lir-ctx-st cx (a64-lir-emit-cmp (cx.state) l r assign)
    is LiTest (l) (r) -> a64-lir-ctx-st cx (a64-lir-emit-test (cx.state) l r assign)
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> a64-lir-ctx-st cx (a64-lir-emit-load (cx.state) dst base off assign)
    is LiRet (v) -> a64-lir-ctx-st cx (a64-lir-emit-ret (cx.state) v assign fr)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> a64-lir-ctx-st cx (a64-lir-emit-call (cx.state) dst name args assign)
    is LiBranch (c) (tblk) (fblk) -> a64-lir-emit-branch cx c tblk fblk nextid
    is LiJump (b) -> a64-lir-emit-jump cx b nextid
    is otherwise -> cx

  a64-lir-emit-insns : A64LirCtx, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, List Location, A64LirFrame, Integer -> A64LirCtx
  a64-lir-emit-insns (cx) (insns) (i) (n) (assign) (fr) (nextid) =
   if i >= n then cx
   else a64-lir-emit-insns (a64-lir-emit-insn cx (list-at insns i) assign fr nextid) insns (i + 1) n assign fr nextid

  a64-lir-next-id : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  a64-lir-next-id (blocks) (i) (n) =
   if (i + 1) >= n then 0 - 1 else ((list-at blocks (i + 1)).id)

  a64-lir-emit-blocks : A64LirCtx, List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List Location, A64LirFrame -> A64LirCtx
  a64-lir-emit-blocks (cx) (blocks) (i) (n) (assign) (fr) =
   if i >= n then cx
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let cx0 = A64LirCtx { state = cx.state, boffs = list-set-at (cx.boffs) (b.id) ((cx.state).insn-count), fixups = cx.fixups }
   in let cx1 = a64-lir-emit-insns cx0 (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) assign fr (a64-lir-next-id blocks i n)
   in a64-lir-emit-blocks cx1 blocks (i + 1) n assign fr

  a64-lir-apply-fixups : A64State, List A64LirFix, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> A64State
  a64-lir-apply-fixups (st) (fixups) (boffs) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then st
   else let fx = list-at fixups i
   in let rel = ((list-at boffs (fx.target)) - (fx.at)) * 4
   in let st1 = if (fx.cond) < 0 then a64-patch-insn st (fx.at) (arm64-b rel) else a64-patch-insn st (fx.at) (arm64-b-cond (fx.cond) rel)
   in a64-lir-apply-fixups st1 fixups boffs (i + 1) n

 The result of an arithmetic function is a vreg the allocator placed, so this
 reads `assign` where the zero-instruction path reads the parameter index. A
 result already sitting in `x0` still emits nothing, and a result in a spill
 slot is loaded out of the frame while the frame is still standing. The load
 must precede the epilogue for that reason, and a result in a callee-saved
 register must be copied out before the restore puts the caller's value back;
 both orderings are what `a64-lir-emit-framed` fixes.

  a64-lir-result-loc : A64State, Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-result-loc (st) (loc) =
   when loc
    is LocReg (pr) ->
     let p = list-at a64-lir-phys pr
     in if p == a64-x0 then st else a64-emit st (arm64-mov a64-x0 p)
    is LocSlot (k) -> a64-emit st (arm64-ldr a64-x0 a64-sp (a64-lir-slot-off k))

  a64-lir-emit-result-alloc : A64State, LirValue, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-result-alloc (st) (v) (assign) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> a64-emit-li st a64-x0 k
    is LvReg (r) -> a64-lir-result-loc st (list-at assign r)

  a64-lir-zero-insn : LirFunc -> Boolean
  a64-lir-zero-insn (f) =
   if f.rets then False
   else if list-length (f.blocks) /= 1 then False
   else list-length ((list-at (f.blocks) 0).insns) == 0

  a64-lir-emit-func : A64State, IRDef, LirFunc, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-func (st) (def) (f) (assign) =
   let st1 = a64-reset-func-state st (def.name) (list-length (def.params))
   in if a64-lir-zero-insn f
      then a64-emit (a64-lir-emit-result-param st1 (f.result)) arm64-ret
      else a64-lir-emit-framed st1 f assign

  a64-lir-frame-of : LirFunc, List Location -> A64LirFrame
  a64-lir-frame-of (f) (assign) =
   let na = list-length assign
   in A64LirFrame {
    nslots = a64-lir-nslots assign 0 na 0,
    nused = a64-lir-nused-callee assign 0 na 0,
    haslr = a64-lir-has-call (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks))
   }

  a64-lir-emit-framed : A64State, LirFunc, List Location -> A64State
  a64-lir-emit-framed (st) (f) (assign) =
   let fr = a64-lir-frame-of f assign
   in let nb = list-length (f.blocks)
   in let ste = a64-lir-emit-entry (a64-lir-emit-prologue st fr) f assign
   in let cx0 = A64LirCtx { state = ste, boffs = lir-int-vec 0 (a64-lir-max-block-id (f.blocks) 0 nb 0) 0 [], fixups = [] }
   in let cxf = a64-lir-emit-blocks cx0 (f.blocks) 0 nb assign fr
   in let stp = a64-lir-apply-fixups (cxf.state) (cxf.fixups) (cxf.boffs) 0 (list-length (cxf.fixups))
   in if f.rets then stp
      else a64-emit (a64-lir-emit-epilogue (a64-lir-emit-result-alloc stp (f.result) assign) fr) arm64-ret

 The offset table is sized by the highest block id present, not by the block
 count, because the two are not the same number: ids are handed out eagerly
 across nested arms and a block that was numbered and then never emitted leaves
 a gap. Indexing by id into a table sized by count would write past its end.

  a64-lir-max-block-id : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  a64-lir-max-block-id (blocks) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc + 1
   else let bid = ((list-at blocks i).id)
   in a64-lir-max-block-id blocks (i + 1) n (if bid > acc then bid else acc)

 That bound is the frame's own size. `arm64-sub-imm` and `arm64-add-imm` take a
 twelve-bit immediate and MASK what they are given, so a frame past 4095 bytes
 would be silently truncated into a stack pointer wrong by four kilobytes. That
 is a miscompile rather than a diagnostic, so the size is refused here instead.

  a64-lir-alloc-ok : LirFunc, List Location -> Boolean
  a64-lir-alloc-ok (f) (assign) =
   if a64-lir-zero-insn f then True
   else a64-lir-frame-bytes (a64-lir-frame-of f assign) <= 4095

  a64-lir-emit-try : A64State, IRDef -> A64LirTry
  a64-lir-emit-try (st) (def) =
   if def.name == "opening" then a64-lir-not-handled st
   else if list-length (def.params) > 8 then a64-lir-not-handled st
   else let r = lower-def-to-lir def
   in if r.ok == False then a64-lir-not-handled st
      else if (r.func).nparams /= list-length (def.params) then a64-lir-not-handled st
      else if a64-lir-selects (r.func) == False then a64-lir-not-handled st
      else if a64-lir-calls-ok st ((r.func).blocks) 0 (list-length ((r.func).blocks)) == False then a64-lir-not-handled st
      else if list-length (lir-check-func (r.func)) > 0 then a64-lir-not-handled st
      else let a = lir-alloc-linscan lir-target-a64 (r.func)
      in if list-length (lir-alloc-check lir-target-a64 (r.func) (a.assign)) > 0 then a64-lir-not-handled st
         else if a64-lir-alloc-ok (r.func) (a.assign) == False then a64-lir-not-handled st
         else A64LirTry { handled = True, state = a64-lir-emit-func st def (r.func) (a.assign) }
